================================================
PART 4 — .gitignore
================================================

Create a file named exactly:

    .gitignore

LOCATION:
    PROJECT ROOT
    (same folder where pom.xml is present)

For the question paper, the .gitignore MUST contain exactly these entries:

    .env
    node_modules/
    target/

Do NOT replace these with placeholder/example names.

Meaning:
    .env            -> ignores .env files
    node_modules/   -> ignores the node_modules directory
    target/         -> ignores Maven's target directory

Then run:

    git status
    git add .gitignore
    git commit -m "Add gitignore"

IMPORTANT:
If target/ was already committed before .gitignore was created, Git will continue
tracking it. To remove target/ from Git tracking while keeping it on your computer:

    git rm -r --cached target/
    git add .gitignore
    git commit -m "Ignore generated files"

Then:
    git status
    git add .
    git commit -m "Initial commit"

IMPORTANT:
If target/ was already committed before adding it to .gitignore,
.gitignore alone will not remove it from Git tracking.

To stop tracking target/ while keeping it on your computer:
    git rm -r --cached target/
    git add .gitignore
    git commit -m "Ignore generated files"

================================================
PART 5 — FIRST GITHUB CONNECTION
================================================

OPTION A — HTTPS (recommended if you have not configured SSH)

1. Check whether a remote already exists:
    git remote -v

2. If NO origin exists:
    git remote add origin https://github.com/<GITHUB_USERNAME>/<GITHUB_REPO>.git

Example:
    git remote add origin https://github.com/VANDANAPUSATHWIKHSAI/cn1.git

3. If origin ALREADY EXISTS, DO NOT run "git remote add origin" again.
   You will get:
       error: remote origin already exists.

Instead, change it:
    git remote set-url origin https://github.com/<GITHUB_USERNAME>/<GITHUB_REPO>.git

4. Verify:
    git remote -v

It should show the repository you actually want to use.

5. Rename the local branch to main:
    git branch -M main

6. Push:
    git push -u origin main

FIRST PUSH / TWO GITHUB ACCOUNTS:
If Git uses the wrong saved GitHub account and gives a 403 such as:
    Permission to OWNER/REPO.git denied to WRONG_USERNAME.

Remove the old GitHub credentials from:
    Control Panel
    -> Credential Manager
    -> Windows Credentials

Remove the saved GitHub credential, then run:
    git push -u origin main

Sign in with the GitHub account that OWNS the target repository or has
permission to push to it.

IMPORTANT:
Git's:
    git config user.name
    git config user.email
and the GitHub account used to authenticate are separate things.

================================================
PART 6 — SSH GITHUB CONNECTION
================================================

If the exam specifically asks for SSH:

Remote format:
    git@github.com:<GITHUB_USERNAME>/<GITHUB_REPO>.git

If origin does not exist:
    git remote add origin git@github.com:<GITHUB_USERNAME>/<GITHUB_REPO>.git

If origin already exists:
    git remote set-url origin git@github.com:<GITHUB_USERNAME>/<GITHUB_REPO>.git

Check:
    git remote -v

Then:
    git branch -M main
    git push -u origin main

If you get:
    git@github.com: Permission denied (publickey).

SSH is not configured for that GitHub account on that laptop.

For a simple setup, switch the remote to HTTPS:
    git remote set-url origin https://github.com/<GITHUB_USERNAME>/<GITHUB_REPO>.git

Then:
    git push -u origin main

================================================
PART 7 — COMMON GIT PUSH ERRORS WE ENCOUNTERED
================================================

ERROR:
    error: src refspec main does not match any

CAUSE:
    Local branch was still called master or there was no main branch.

FIX:
    git branch -M main
    git push -u origin main

ERROR:
    error: remote origin already exists.

CAUSE:
    origin is already configured.

FIX:
    git remote -v

If it is the WRONG repository:
    git remote set-url origin <CORRECT_REPOSITORY_URL>

Do NOT use:
    git remote add origin ...
again.

ERROR:
    Permission to <owner>/<repo>.git denied to <another-account>.

CAUSE:
    GitHub authenticated with another account.

FIX:
    Remove the old GitHub credential from Windows Credential Manager,
    then push again and authenticate with the account that owns/has access
    to the repository.

ERROR:
    git@github.com: Permission denied (publickey).

CAUSE:
    SSH key is missing/not configured for the GitHub account.

FIX:
    Either configure SSH properly, OR use HTTPS:
    git remote set-url origin https://github.com/<USERNAME>/<REPO>.git

